2. Mathematical Foundations

2.1 Three-Dimensional Identity Algebra

Definition 1 (Cryptographic Identity).

I_{\text{crypto}} = (\text{id}, \; \sigma, \; \text{ver})

where \text{id} is an Ed25519 keypair stored in the TPM, \sigma is the read-only-at-runtime identity specification, and \text{ver} is the identity version. The private key never leaves the TPM. The cryptographic identity is device-bound and non-exportable.

Definition 2 (Continuity Identity).

I_{\text{cont}} = (\beta, \; U_t, \; G_t, \; \text{sig}(M_t), \; P_t)

where \beta are behavioral parameters, U_t is the user model, G_t are goals, \text{sig}(M_t) is the memory signature, and P_t is the authorization policy state.

Definition 3 (Legal Identity).

I_{\text{legal}} = (\text{AgentID}_{\text{MS}}, \; \text{Blueprint}_{\text{Entra}})

where \text{AgentID}_{\text{MS}} is the Microsoft Entra Agent ID and \text{Blueprint}_{\text{Entra}} is the parent blueprint credential. An agent identity blueprint is a Microsoft Entra service principal that holds credentials and governance policies; individual agent identities created from it inherit governance and do not hold their own long-lived credentials.

Definition 4 (Full Identity Projection).

\pi_I(S_t^{P}) = (I_{\text{crypto}}, \; I_{\text{cont}}, \; I_{\text{legal}})

Orthogonality Conditions.

\pi_{I_{\text{crypto}}}(S_{t+1}^{P}) = \pi_{I_{\text{crypto}}}(S_t^{P})

\pi_{I_{\text{cont}}}(S_{t+1}^{P}) \approx \pi_{I_{\text{cont}}}(S_t^{P})

\pi_{I_{\text{legal}}}(S_{t+1}^{P}) = \pi_{I_{\text{legal}}}(S_t^{P})

2.2 Platform Premises for Orthogonality

Theorem 1 is not a pure mathematical result; it is conditional on platform guarantees. We state these as premises.

Premise P1 (TPM Non-Exportability). The TPM 2.0 specification requires that private keys generated within the TPM are non-exportable and that cryptographic operations occur within the TPM boundary. Windows 11 mandates TPM 2.0 for all new devices.

Premise P2 (PCR Binding). The TPM stores platform configuration registers (PCRs) that capture the measured boot state. A TPM quote is a signed assertion over selected PCR values, and Azure Attestation verifies the quote against a policy asserting expected system measurements.

Premise P3 (Entra Tenant Boundary). Agent identity blueprints are tenant-scoped directory objects. Provisioning, credential rotation, and deletion are administrative operations that do not traverse the agent's memory or execution plane.

Premise P4 (Storage Plane Separation). The persistent state plane is stored outside the Windows user profile and is not accessible to the transient execution environment.

Theorem 1 (Identity Orthogonality). Under Premises P1–P4, the three identity dimensions I_{\text{crypto}}, I_{\text{cont}}, and I_{\text{legal}} are pairwise orthogonal under the perturbation operators \mathcal{M}, \mathcal{R}, \mathcal{E}, and \mathcal{X}.

Proof. Orthogonality is established by proving that no perturbation operator can simultaneously affect two dimensions through a shared mechanism.

Step 1: I_{\text{crypto}} \perp I_{\text{cont}}. I_{\text{crypto}} is a TPM-resident Ed25519 keypair. By P1, the private key is non-exportable and never enters the computation plane X_t. By P2, the key is bound to PCRs representing the measured boot state. I_{\text{cont}} is computed from memory content, user model parameters, goal distributions, and policy state—none of which are inputs to TPM key generation. Therefore no memory operation, model substitution, or environmental change can modify I_{\text{crypto}} without explicit TPM owner authorization.

Step 2: I_{\text{cont}} \perp I_{\text{legal}}. I_{\text{legal}} is determined by Entra provisioning. By P3, Entra provisioning is an administrative operation that does not traverse the agent's memory or execution plane. I_{\text{cont}} is a function of runtime memory, user model, and goals. No perturbation operator can simultaneously modify Entra provisioning state and memory content through a shared mechanism.

Step 3: I_{\text{crypto}} \perp I_{\text{legal}}. I_{\text{crypto}} is device-bound and TPM-resident. I_{\text{legal}} is tenant-bound and Entra-resident. The binding between them is the attestation A_t = \text{Sign}_{\text{TPM}}(h(I_{\text{crypto}} \parallel \text{AgentID}_{\text{MS}})), a one-way derivation. Deletion of \text{AgentID}_{\text{MS}} does not affect I_{\text{crypto}}. Deletion of I_{\text{crypto}} (via TPM clear) does not affect \text{AgentID}_{\text{MS}}, but the attestation becomes invalid. ∎

Corollary 1 (Cryptographic Identity Survives Account Deletion).

\pi_{I_{\text{crypto}}}(\mathcal{D}(S_t^{P})) = \pi_{I_{\text{crypto}}}(S_t^{P})

where \mathcal{D} is the operation of deleting the Windows agent account or Entra Agent ID.

Proof. By P4, I_{\text{crypto}}, M_t, P_t, and E_t are stored in the persistent state plane, outside the transient execution environment. Deletion of the Windows agent account removes X_t, including the Windows SID and Entra session token. The TPM keypair remains accessible because access is mediated by TPM owner authorization, not Windows account credentials. ∎

2.3 Two-Plane State Model

Definition 5 (Two-Plane State). The agent occupies:

S_t = (S_t^{P}, \; X_t)

Persistent State Plane:

S_t^{P} = (I_{\text{crypto}}, \; I_{\text{cont}}, \; M_t, \; U_t, \; W_t^{P}, \; G_t, \; P_t, \; E_t, \; A_t)

Transient Computational State:

X_t = (C_t, \; \text{ctx}_t, \; W_t^{X}, \; \mathcal{R}_t, \; \text{AgentID}_{\text{MS}})

Invariant 1 (Identity Immutability). For all t:

\pi_{I_{\text{crypto}}}(S_{t+1}^{P}) = \pi_{I_{\text{crypto}}}(S_t^{P})

except during an explicit, user-authorized identity version transition:

\sigma_k \to \sigma_{k+1} \implies \text{Ver}(I_{\text{crypto}}) \to \text{Ver}(I_{\text{crypto}}) + 1

Invariant 2 (Continuity Boundedness). For any perturbation operator \mathcal{P} \in \{\mathcal{M}, \mathcal{R}, \mathcal{E}, \mathcal{X}\}:

d(\pi_{I_{\text{cont}}}(S_{t+1}^{P}), \; \pi_{I_{\text{cont}}}(S_t^{P})) \leq \delta_{\text{cont}}

where d is a divergence metric on continuity identity components. We instantiate d concretely in §5.1.

