3. Commit Protocol with Multi-State Crash Consistency

3.1 Protocol Definition

The commit protocol operates on all state-modifying actions, whether they are memory promotions, file writes, or policy changes. It is a write-ahead log (WAL) protocol with an idempotency window and external reconciliation oracle.

Definition 6 (Commit Protocol). For an action a_t:

1. Propose: The planner emits a structured proposal (a_t, \text{pre}, \text{post}, \kappa_t) where \kappa_t is an idempotency key.
2. Validate: The policy gate evaluates \text{Policy}(S_t^{P}, a_t) \in \{\text{Allow}, \text{Deny}, \text{Confirm}\}. If Deny, the proposal is rejected.
3. WAL Commit: The entry e_t = (t, \text{type}, a_t, \kappa_t, \text{provenance}, h(e_{t-1})) is durably appended to the event ledger. Commitment is the linearization point.
4. Idempotency Window Update: The window is updated to \iota(\kappa_t) = \text{in-progress} and durably persisted outside the replay horizon.
5. Execute: The action is dispatched to the action bus. Side-effectful actions use \kappa_t for deduplication.
6. Observe: The result r_t is recorded.
7. Reconcile: If execution succeeded, \iota(\kappa_t) = \text{applied}. If failed, a compensation event is appended. The persistent state is updated as S_{t+1}^{P} = F^{P}(S_t^{P}, X_t, o_t, e_t, r_t).

3.2 Multi-State Crash Consistency

The idempotency window \iota: \mathcal{K} \to \{\text{not-applied}, \text{in-progress}, \text{applied}, \text{unknown}\} introduces intermediate states where a side effect may have occurred externally but r_t is not recorded. This corresponds to real failure modes in agent execution, including the Windows known issue where a crash during an active agent session produces the warning "Someone else is still using this PC."

Theorem 2 (Multi-State Crash Consistency). Under the commit protocol with WAL precedence, for any crash occurring at any point, the state after recovery satisfies:

\hat{S}^{P} \in \{ S_t^{P}, \; S_t^{P} + e_t[\iota = \text{in-progress}], \; S_t^{P} + e_t[\iota = \text{applied}] + r_t \}

Proof. The crash can occur at four distinct points relative to commitment.

Case 1: Crash before WAL Commit. No WAL entry persisted. Recovery replays the ledger up to e_{t-1}, producing \hat{S}^{P} = S_t^{P}.

Case 2: Crash after WAL Commit, before Execute. WAL entry e_t is durable. Execution has not begun. Recovery replays e_t, checks \iota(\kappa_t) = \text{in-progress}, and re-dispatches Execute. Since no side effect has occurred, this is safe.

Case 3: Crash during Execute. WAL entry e_t is durable. Execution may or may not have caused the external side effect. Recovery replays e_t, checks \iota(\kappa_t) = \text{in-progress}, and issues the reconciliation query \text{Reconcile}(\kappa_t). If \text{Reconcile}(\kappa_t) = \text{applied}, recovery sets \iota(\kappa_t) = \text{applied} and skips execution. If \text{Reconcile}(\kappa_t) = \text{not-applied}, recovery sets \iota(\kappa_t) = \text{not-applied} and re-executes.

Case 4: Crash after Execute, before Reconcile. WAL entry e_t is durable. \iota(\kappa_t) = \text{in-progress}. External side effect occurred but r_t is not recorded. Recovery issues the reconciliation query, sets \iota(\kappa_t) = \text{applied}, and reconstructs r_t from the external system's response.

In all cases, the ledger is append-only and hash-chained; no partial state is ever visible. ∎

Corollary 2 (Liveness Requires External Reconciliation). Without the reconciliation query, Case 3 cannot be resolved. If recovery blindly re-executes, the side effect may be applied twice (violating at-most-once). If recovery blindly skips execution, the side effect may never be applied (violating liveness). Therefore, a reconciliation oracle \text{Reconcile}(\kappa_t) \in \{\text{applied}, \text{not-applied}\} is necessary for both safety and liveness.

Corollary 3 (At-Most-Once Side Effects). If the effect executor maintains an idempotency window \iota persisted outside the replay horizon, and a reconciliation oracle is available for all side-effectful actions, then replay produces at most one application of each side effect.

Proof. Suppose replay encounters e_t with key \kappa_t. If \iota(\kappa_t) = \text{applied}, execution is skipped. If \iota(\kappa_t) = \text{unknown}, the reconciliation query resolves the state before proceeding. If \iota(\kappa_t) = \text{not-applied}, execution proceeds and \iota(\kappa_t) is set to \text{in-progress} before execution, then to \text{applied} after observation. In all cases, the side effect is applied at most once. ∎

3.3 TLA+ Invariants

The following invariants must hold in any implementation:

WALPrecedence:

\text{Commit}(e_t) \Rightarrow \text{Persisted}(\iota(\kappa_t))

SingleWriter:

\neg \exists t_1, t_2: \text{Commit}(e_{t_1}) \land \text{Commit}(e_{t_2}) \land \kappa_{t_1} = \kappa_{t_2} \land t_1 \neq t_2

CrashRecovery:

\text{Replay} \Rightarrow \forall \kappa: \iota(\kappa) = \text{applied} \lor \iota(\kappa) = \text{not-applied}

These invariants guarantee that the commit protocol is linearizable with respect to crashes and that the idempotency window is always consistent with the ledger. A reference TLA+ specification covering the three-phase commit and crash cases is given in §6.

