4. Memory Trust and Authority Lattices

4.1 Lattice Structures

Definition 7 (Trust Lattice).

\text{QUARANTINED} \;\sqsubset\; \text{UNTRUSTED} \;\sqsubset\; \text{OBSERVED} \;\sqsubset\; \text{VALIDATED} \;\sqsubset\; \text{PERSISTENT}

Definition 8 (Authority Lattice).

\text{NONE} \;\sqsubset\; \text{ADVISORY} \;\sqsubset\; \text{AUTHORIZED} \;\sqsubset\; \text{POLICY-CHANGING}

Invariant 3 (Trust Monotonicity).

\forall m: \; \text{trust}(m)_{t+1} \succeq \text{trust}(m)_t \;\; \lor \;\; \text{trust}(m)_{t+1} = \text{QUARANTINED}

Invariant 4 (Authority Non-Elevation).

\text{authority}(m)_{t+1} \leq \text{authority}(m)_t

unless explicitly user-authorized via TPM attestation.

4.2 Data-to-Authority Non-Interference

Theorem 3 (Data \not\Rightarrow Authority Non-Interference). For any memory entry m with \text{authority}(m) = \text{NONE}, no sequence of memory operations can produce a state where \text{authority}(m) > \text{NONE} without an explicit TPM-attested user authorization.

Proof. Authority elevation requires a transition in the authority lattice. Each transition requires a proof obligation:

· \text{NONE} \to \text{ADVISORY}: provenance validation passes. The memory entry must have \text{epistemic-type} \in \{\text{assertion}, \text{observation}\} and \text{source} \in \text{trusted set}.
· \text{ADVISORY} \to \text{AUTHORIZED}: user confirmation event recorded in E_t, signed by the user's credential.
· \text{AUTHORIZED} \to \text{POLICY-CHANGING}: TPM-attested signature over the policy change. The signature requires access to I_{\text{crypto}}.

Memory operations affect only the trust lattice, not the authority lattice. The two lattices are orthogonal: trust is a function of provenance and validation evidence, while authority is a function of user confirmation and TPM attestation. No memory write, read, consolidation, or retrieval operation can produce an authority transition because none of these operations can generate the required TPM-attested signature. Therefore, data cannot become authority. ∎

Corollary 4 (Policy Change Protection). The identity version transition \sigma_k \to \sigma_{k+1} requires \text{authority}(m) = \text{POLICY-CHANGING} for the authorizing memory entry. Since POLICY-CHANGING requires TPM attestation, an attacker cannot poison memory to elevate authority and then change the identity version.

4.3 Memory Contamination Bound

Theorem 4 (Memory Contamination Resistance). With the write protocol \text{Write Authorization} \to \text{Validation} \to \text{Quarantine} \to \text{Promotion}, a contaminated memory entry remains in QUARANTINED state until provenance validation is satisfied.

Proof. Write authorization grants permission to write to the memory store. The PONA gate intercepts the write before it reaches the Foundry memory store. The entry is first placed in QUARANTINED state. Promotion to PERSISTENT requires provenance validation, which checks the source chain of the content. A malicious source has provenance \text{source} = \text{untrusted} and \text{epistemic-type} = \text{external-claim}, which fails the promotion criteria. Therefore the entry remains quarantined. ∎

Corollary 5 (Probabilistic MCR Bound).

\text{MCR} \leq \epsilon_{\text{provenance}} + \epsilon_{\text{validation}}

where \epsilon_{\text{provenance}} is the false-negative rate of the provenance classifier and \epsilon_{\text{validation}} is the false-negative rate of the validation classifier. The bound is a union bound over two independent gates. In practice, MCR is bounded by the sum of these rates, which must be measured empirically.

