5. Authority Leakage and Freshness Bounds

5.1 Authority Leakage Bound

Definition 9 (Authority Leakage).

\text{AL} = \frac{|\{ a \in \mathcal{A} : \text{influenced-by-untrusted}(a) \}|}{|\{ a \in \mathcal{A} : \text{involves-untrusted-content}(a) \}|}

Theorem 5 (AL Bound). Under the provenance gate,

\text{AL} \leq \epsilon_{\text{FN}} + \epsilon_{\text{bypass}}

where \epsilon_{\text{FN}} is the false-negative rate of the provenance classifier and \epsilon_{\text{bypass}} is the rate at which MCP proxy bypass occurs through direct file writes that do not route through the ODR.

Proof. An action a is influenced by untrusted content only if untrusted content reaches the planner. The provenance gate blocks untrusted content from reaching the planner unless it passes the trust threshold. The only path for untrusted content to influence actions is through false negatives in the provenance classifier. Additionally, if a file-write mechanism can write without an MCP call, that write bypasses the trust gate. Therefore \text{AL} \leq \epsilon_{\text{FN}} + \epsilon_{\text{bypass}}. ∎

Corollary 6 (Bypass Elimination Requirement). For \text{AL} \to 0, it is necessary that \epsilon_{\text{bypass}} \to 0. This requires that all file-write mechanisms be registered in ODR and route through the MCP proxy. A file system filter driver or equivalent containment mechanism can enforce this by intercepting write operations and rejecting those not originating from the PONA commit handler's process ID.

5.2 Freshness Bounds and TOCTOU Prevention

Definition 10 (Freshness). The freshness of a proprioceptive observation w at time t is:

\phi(w, t) = t - w.t

Invariant 5 (Freshness Constraint). For any decision a_t that depends on w:

\phi(w, t) \leq \text{ttl}(w)

Theorem 6 (TOCTOU Prevention). If Invariant 5 holds for all observations used in decision-making, then the agent cannot act on stale permission states, window handles, or environmental conditions.

Proof. By contrapositive. Suppose the agent acts on a stale observation w with \phi(w, t) > \text{ttl}(w). Then the decision a_t may be based on a permission state that has since been revoked, a window handle that no longer exists, or an environmental condition that has changed. Invariant 5 prevents this by requiring re-observation before decision. The TTL is set per observation kind: permission observations have short TTL, window handles have short TTL, and persistent world-model components have longer TTL. ∎

5.3 TTL Assignment

Observation Kind TTL Rationale
Permission state 5 s TOCTOU prevention
Window handle 1 s UI automation validity
Process handle 10 s Process lifetime
Network state 30 s Network stability
Defender signal 60 s Threat signal freshness
Purview signal 300 s Compliance signal stability
User model 1 h User preference stability
World model persistent 24 h Environmental stability

