9. Known Limitations and Open Problems

9.1 Limitations

1. TPM clearing. A device reset or TPM owner clear removes I_{\text{crypto}}. Recovery requires threshold escrow. FROST-Ed25519 provides threshold signatures but requires a distributed key generation protocol. Integration with TPM-backed key storage remains an open engineering problem.
2. Reconciliation oracle availability. At-most-once semantics for arbitrary external systems require the external system to support a reconciliation query. For systems that do not, PONA can only provide at-most-once by refusing to retry and requiring human reconciliation.
3. Provenance classifier error. The provenance classifier is not perfect. Adversarial content that evades classification can reach the planner. The bound \text{AL} \leq \epsilon_{\text{FN}} + \epsilon_{\text{bypass}} is only as good as the classifier.
4. File system bypass. Direct file writes that bypass ODR registration increase \epsilon_{\text{bypass}}. A file system filter driver is required to enforce ODR-only write paths. Without it, \epsilon_{\text{bypass}} > 0.
5. Model provider trust. The LLM provider can observe context. PONA does not protect against model provider leakage. This requires contractual and technical controls outside the architecture.
6. Cross-device migration. I_{\text{crypto}} is device-bound. Migrating identity to a new device requires either TPM key export (not supported) or re-attestation with a new key. A secure migration protocol is needed.

9.2 Open Problems

1. Optimal TTL assignment. The TTL for each observation kind is currently set by heuristic. Optimal TTL depends on the rate of environmental change and the cost of stale observation. A formal framework for TTL optimization is needed.
2. Threshold key generation without trusted dealer. FROST-Ed25519 provides threshold signatures but requires a distributed key generation protocol. Integration with TPM-backed key storage is an open problem.
3. Formal verification of the full architecture. The TLA+ specification covers the commit protocol. A full formal verification of the identity layer, memory lattice, and MCP proxy integration remains future work.
4. Empirical validation. The metrics MSC, AL, MCR, FVR, and RSR are defined but not yet measured. Running the experimental matrix against baselines B0–B4 with public adversarial datasets is the next step.

---

10. Conclusion

PONA v0.6 strengthens the architecture relative to v0.5 in six specific ways:

1. Platform premises made explicit. Theorem 1 is now conditional on stated platform guarantees (TPM non-exportability, PCR binding, Entra tenant boundary, storage plane separation). The theorem is meaningful because it identifies exactly which platform properties are load-bearing.
2. Positioning clarified. PONA is positioned as a governance and invariance layer, not a replacement for Microsoft's agent infrastructure. A side-by-side mapping of PONA components to Microsoft surfaces is provided.
3. Identity binding hardened. The attestation mechanism uses TPM quotes verified against Azure Attestation, with measured boot PCR binding so that kernel compromise is detectable and forces fail-closed.
4. Commit protocol production-ready. The TLA+ invariants (WALPrecedence, SingleWriter, CrashRecovery) are specified. The reconciliation oracle is promoted to a theorem-level requirement, and the fail-closed default for tools without reconciliation oracles is stated.
5. Bypass surface addressed. ODR registration via odr.exe mcp add and file-system filter driver requirements are specified. The bypass rate \epsilon_{\text{bypass}} is identified as the load-bearing element for \text{AL} \to 0.
6. Memory and authority integration. The trust lattice is integrated with Foundry's existing extraction/consolidation pipeline rather than duplicating it. POLICY-CHANGING elevation requires TPM-signed user confirmation recorded in E_t.

The central hypothesis remains experimentally testable:

\boxed{\text{Persistent identity can be engineered independently of}}

\boxed{\text{cognitive substrate, environmental state, adversarial observation,}}

\boxed{\text{and Microsoft account lifecycle.}}

If validated, this establishes the foundation for a persistent artificial agent whose identity and governed state survive model replacement, operating-system restart, changing digital environments, adversarial observation, and platform account management—while keeping cognitive capability separate from authority. The architecture sits atop Microsoft's production agent infrastructure, providing the invariance and crash-consistency guarantees that the platform does not yet offer.

