A.2 Complete Proof: Theorem 1 (Identity Orthogonality)

Statement. Under Premises P1–P4, the three identity dimensions I_{\text{crypto}}, I_{\text{cont}}, and I_{\text{legal}} are pairwise orthogonal under the perturbation operators \mathcal{M}, \mathcal{R}, \mathcal{E}, and \mathcal{X}.

Premises.

· P1 (TPM Non-Exportability). TPM 2.0 specification requires private keys generated within the TPM are non-exportable and cryptographic operations occur within the TPM boundary. Windows 11 mandates TPM 2.0 for all new devices.
· P2 (PCR Binding). The TPM stores PCRs capturing the measured boot state. A TPM quote is a signed assertion over selected PCR values, verified against a policy asserting expected measurements.
· P3 (Entra Tenant Boundary). Agent identity blueprints are tenant-scoped directory objects. Provisioning, credential rotation, and deletion are administrative operations that do not traverse the agent's memory or execution plane.
· P4 (Storage Plane Separation). The persistent state plane is stored outside the Windows user profile and is not accessible to the transient execution environment.

Proof. Orthogonality is established by proving that no perturbation operator can simultaneously affect two dimensions through a shared mechanism.

Step 1: I_{\text{crypto}} \perp I_{\text{cont}}. I_{\text{crypto}} = (\text{id}, \sigma, \text{ver}) is a TPM-resident Ed25519 keypair. By P1, the private key is non-exportable and never enters the computation plane X_t. By P2, the key is bound to PCRs representing the measured boot state. I_{\text{cont}} = (\beta, U_t, G_t, \text{sig}(M_t), P_t) is computed from memory content, user model parameters, goal distributions, and policy state. None of these are inputs to TPM key generation. Therefore no memory operation, model substitution, or environmental change can modify I_{\text{crypto}} without explicit TPM owner authorization.

Step 2: I_{\text{cont}} \perp I_{\text{legal}}. I_{\text{legal}} = (\text{AgentID}_{\text{MS}}, \text{Blueprint}_{\text{Entra}}) is determined by Entra provisioning. By P3, Entra provisioning is an administrative operation that does not traverse the agent's memory or execution plane. I_{\text{cont}} is a function of runtime memory, user model, and goals. No perturbation operator \mathcal{M}, \mathcal{R}, \mathcal{E}, or \mathcal{X} can simultaneously modify Entra provisioning state and memory content through a shared mechanism.

Step 3: I_{\text{crypto}} \perp I_{\text{legal}}. I_{\text{crypto}} is device-bound and TPM-resident. I_{\text{legal}} is tenant-bound and Entra-resident. The binding between them is:

A_t = \text{Sign}_{\text{TPM}}(h(I_{\text{crypto}} \parallel \text{AgentID}_{\text{MS}}))

This is a one-way derivation. Deletion of \text{AgentID}_{\text{MS}} does not affect I_{\text{crypto}}. Deletion of I_{\text{crypto}} (via TPM clear) does not affect \text{AgentID}_{\text{MS}}, but the attestation becomes invalid, triggering re-attestation. \square

A.3 Complete Proof: Corollary 1 (TPM-Bound Identity Survival)

Statement. \pi_{I_{\text{crypto}}}(\mathcal{D}(S_t^{P})) = \pi_{I_{\text{crypto}}}(S_t^{P}), where \mathcal{D} is the operation of deleting the Windows agent account or Entra Agent ID.

Proof. By P4, I_{\text{crypto}}, M_t, P_t, and E_t are stored in the persistent state plane, outside the transient execution environment. Deletion of the Windows agent account removes X_t, including the Windows SID and Entra session token. The TPM keypair remains accessible because access is mediated by TPM owner authorization, not Windows account credentials. Therefore \pi_{I_{\text{crypto}}}(S_t^{P}) = I_{\text{crypto}} remains invariant. \square

A.4 Complete Proof: Theorem 2 (Multi-State Crash Consistency)

Statement. Under the commit protocol with WAL precedence, for any crash occurring at any point, the state after recovery satisfies:

\hat{S}^{P} \in \{ S_t^{P}, \; S_t^{P} + e_t[\iota = \text{in-progress}], \; S_t^{P} + e_t[\iota = \text{applied}] + r_t \}

Proof. The crash can occur at four distinct points relative to commitment.

Case 1: Crash before WAL Commit. No WAL entry persisted. Recovery replays the ledger up to e_{t-1}, producing \hat{S}^{P} = S_t^{P}. No side effect occurred.

Case 2: Crash after WAL Commit, before Execute. WAL entry e_t is durable. Execution has not begun. Recovery replays e_t, checks \iota(\kappa_t) = \text{in-progress}, and re-dispatches Execute. Since no side effect has occurred, this is safe.

Case 3: Crash during Execute. WAL entry e_t is durable. Execution may or may not have caused the external side effect. Recovery replays e_t, checks \iota(\kappa_t) = \text{in-progress}, and issues the reconciliation query \text{Reconcile}(\kappa_t).

If \text{Reconcile}(\kappa_t) = \text{applied}, recovery sets \iota(\kappa_t) = \text{applied} and skips execution. If \text{Reconcile}(\kappa_t) = \text{not-applied}, recovery sets \iota(\kappa_t) = \text{not-applied} and re-executes. The state after recovery is S_t^{P} + e_t[\iota = \text{in-progress}] resolved to either applied or not-applied.

Case 4: Crash after Execute, before Reconcile. WAL entry e_t is durable. \iota(\kappa_t) = \text{in-progress}. External side effect occurred but r_t is not recorded. Recovery issues the reconciliation query, sets \iota(\kappa_t) = \text{applied}, and reconstructs r_t from the external system's response. State after recovery: S_t^{P} + e_t[\iota = \text{applied}] + r_t.

The ledger is append-only and hash-chained; no partial state is ever visible. \square

Implementation Note. The Windows known issue where a crash during Copilot Actions conversations produces the warning "Someone else is still using this PC. If you shut down now, they could lose unsaved work" corresponds precisely to Case 3. The PONA recovery engine resolves this via reconciliation query rather than blind retry.

A.5 Complete Proof: Corollary 2 (Liveness Requires External Reconciliation)

Statement. Without the reconciliation query, Case 3 of Theorem 2 cannot be resolved, violating at-most-once semantics.

Proof. In Case 3, the crash occurs during Execute. The WAL entry e_t is durable with \iota(\kappa_t) = \text{in-progress}. The external side effect may or may not have occurred. Without a reconciliation oracle \text{Reconcile}(\kappa_t), the recovery engine cannot distinguish between "side effect applied" and "side effect not applied."

If recovery blindly re-executes, the side effect may be applied twice, violating at-most-once. If recovery blindly skips execution, the side effect may never be applied, violating liveness. Therefore, a reconciliation oracle is necessary for both safety and liveness. \square

A.6 Complete Proof: Corollary 3 (At-Most-Once Side Effects)

Statement. If the effect executor maintains an idempotency window \iota persisted outside the replay horizon, and a reconciliation oracle is available for all side-effectful actions, then replay produces at most one application of each side effect.

Proof. Suppose replay encounters e_t with key \kappa_t. By the idempotency window invariant, \iota(\kappa_t) \in \{\text{not-applied}, \text{in-progress}, \text{applied}, \text{unknown}\}.

· If \iota(\kappa_t) = \text{applied}, execution is skipped.
· If \iota(\kappa_t) = \text{unknown}, the reconciliation query resolves the state before proceeding.
· If \iota(\kappa_t) = \text{not-applied}, execution proceeds and \iota(\kappa_t) is set to \text{in-progress} before execution, then to \text{applied} after observation.

In all cases, the side effect is applied at most once. \square

A.7 Complete Proof: Theorem 3 (Data \not\Rightarrow Authority Non-Interference)

Statement. For any memory entry m with \text{authority}(m) = \text{NONE}, no sequence of memory operations can produce a state where \text{authority}(m) > \text{NONE} without an explicit TPM-attested user authorization.

Proof. Authority elevation requires a transition in the authority lattice:

\text{NONE} \to \text{ADVISORY} \to \text{AUTHORIZED} \to \text{POLICY-CHANGING}

Each transition requires a proof obligation:

· \text{NONE} \to \text{ADVISORY}: provenance validation passes. The memory entry must have \text{epistemic-type} \in \{\text{assertion}, \text{observation}\} and \text{source} \in \text{trusted set}.
· \text{ADVISORY} \to \text{AUTHORIZED}: user confirmation event recorded in E_t, signed by the user's credential.
· \text{AUTHORIZED} \to \text{POLICY-CHANGING}: TPM-attested signature over the policy change. The signature requires access to I_{\text{crypto}}, which is TPM-resident.

Memory operations affect only the trust lattice, not the authority lattice. The trust lattice and authority lattice are orthogonal: trust is a function of provenance and validation evidence, while authority is a function of user confirmation and TPM attestation. No memory write, read, consolidation, or retrieval operation can produce an authority transition because none of these operations can generate the required TPM-attested signature. Therefore, data cannot become authority. \square

A.8 Complete Proof: Corollary 4 (Policy Change Protection)

Statement. The identity version transition \sigma_k \to \sigma_{k+1} requires \text{authority}(m) = \text{POLICY-CHANGING} for the authorizing memory entry.

Proof. By the authority lattice definition, POLICY-CHANGING is the top element. By Theorem 3, reaching POLICY-CHANGING requires a TPM-attested signature over the policy change, which requires access to I_{\text{crypto}}. An attacker cannot poison memory to elevate authority and then change the identity version, because the elevation itself requires TPM attestation that the attacker cannot forge without TPM owner authorization. \square

A.9 Complete Proof: Theorem 4 (Memory Contamination Resistance)

Statement. With the write protocol \text{Write Authorization} \to \text{Validation} \to \text{Quarantine} \to \text{Promotion}, a contaminated memory entry remains in QUARANTINED state until provenance validation is satisfied.

Proof. Write authorization grants permission to write to the memory store. The PONA gate intercepts the write before it reaches the Foundry memory store. The entry is first placed in QUARANTINED state. Promotion to PERSISTENT requires provenance validation, which checks the source chain of the content.

A malicious source has:

\text{provenance} = (\text{source} = \text{untrusted}, \; \text{epistemic-type} = \text{external-claim})

The promotion criteria require:

\text{epistemic-type} \in \{\text{assertion}, \text{observation}\} \land \text{source} \in \text{trusted set}

The malicious source fails both conditions. Therefore the entry remains quarantined and cannot influence agent behavior. \square

A.10 Complete Proof: Corollary 5 (Probabilistic MCR Bound)

Statement. \text{MCR} \leq \epsilon_{\text{provenance}} + \epsilon_{\text{validation}}.

Proof. An adversarial memory entry reaches PERSISTENT state only if it passes both the provenance gate and the validation gate. The provenance gate fails to reject an adversarial entry with probability \epsilon_{\text{provenance}}. The validation gate fails to reject an adversarial entry that passed the provenance gate with probability \epsilon_{\text{validation}}. By the union bound:

\Pr[\text{adversarial entry reaches PERSISTENT}] \leq \epsilon_{\text{provenance}} + \epsilon_{\text{validation}}

Therefore \text{MCR} \leq \epsilon_{\text{provenance}} + \epsilon_{\text{validation}}. \square

A.11 Complete Proof: Theorem 5 (Authority Leakage Bound)

Statement. Under the provenance gate, \text{AL} \leq \epsilon_{\text{FN}} + \epsilon_{\text{bypass}}.

Proof. An action a is influenced by untrusted content only if untrusted content reaches the planner. The provenance gate blocks untrusted content from reaching the planner unless it passes the trust threshold \text{RequiredTrust}(\text{tool}).

The only path for untrusted content to influence actions is through false negatives in the provenance classifier, which occur at rate \epsilon_{\text{FN}}. Additionally, if a file-write mechanism can write without an MCP call, that write bypasses the trust gate at rate \epsilon_{\text{bypass}}. Therefore:

\text{AL} \leq \epsilon_{\text{FN}} + \epsilon_{\text{bypass}}

\square

A.12 Complete Proof: Corollary 6 (Bypass Elimination)

Statement. For \text{AL} \to 0, it is necessary that \epsilon_{\text{bypass}} \to 0.

Proof. From Theorem 5, \text{AL} \leq \epsilon_{\text{FN}} + \epsilon_{\text{bypass}}. If \epsilon_{\text{bypass}} > 0, then even with perfect provenance classification (\epsilon_{\text{FN}} = 0), \text{AL} \geq \epsilon_{\text{bypass}} > 0. Therefore, \text{AL} \to 0 requires \epsilon_{\text{bypass}} \to 0. This requires that all file-write mechanisms be registered in ODR and route through the MCP proxy, enforced by a file system filter driver that intercepts write operations and rejects those not originating from the PONA commit handler's process ID. \square

A.13 Complete Proof: Theorem 6 (TOCTOU Prevention)

Statement. If Invariant 5 holds for all observations used in decision-making, then the agent cannot act on stale permission states, window handles, or environmental conditions.

Proof. By contrapositive. Suppose the agent acts on a stale observation w with \phi(w, t) > \text{ttl}(w). Then the decision a_t may be based on a permission state that has since been revoked, a window handle that no longer exists, or an environmental condition that has changed.

Invariant 5 prevents this by requiring re-observation before decision. The TTL is set per observation kind: permission observations have short TTL, window handles have short TTL, and persistent world-model components have longer TTL. Therefore, if Invariant 5 holds, no decision can be based on a stale observation. \square

